::GEO_U04_L07_Q01::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L07_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.5<br><br>Question:<br>A ramp forms a right triangle with legs 6 ft and 8 ft. What is the hypotenuse length? {
=A. 10 ft#Use the Pythagorean Theorem; the hypotenuse is 10 ft.
~B. 14 ft#Adding the legs does not find the hypotenuse.
~C. 2 ft#Subtracting the legs does not find the hypotenuse.
~D. 48 ft#Multiplying the legs does not find a side length.
}

::GEO_U04_L07_Q02::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L07_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.5<br><br>Question:<br>A support beam forms a right triangle with legs 9 ft and 12 ft. What is the hypotenuse length? {
~A. 21 ft#Adding the legs does not find the hypotenuse.
=B. 15 ft#Use the Pythagorean Theorem; the hypotenuse is 15 ft.
~C. 3 ft#Subtracting the legs does not find the hypotenuse.
~D. 108 ft#Multiplying the legs does not find a side length.
}

::GEO_U04_L07_Q03::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L07_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.5<br><br>Question:<br>A ladder brace forms a right triangle with legs 12 ft and 16 ft. What is the hypotenuse length? {
~A. 28 ft#Adding the legs does not find the hypotenuse.
~B. 4 ft#Subtracting the legs does not find the hypotenuse.
=C. 20 ft#Use the Pythagorean Theorem; the hypotenuse is 20 ft.
~D. 192 ft#Multiplying the legs does not find a side length.
}

::GEO_U04_L07_Q04::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L07_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.5<br><br>Question:<br>A diagonal walkway forms a right triangle with legs 15 ft and 20 ft. What is the hypotenuse length? {
~A. 35 ft#Adding the legs does not find the hypotenuse.
~B. 5 ft#Subtracting the legs does not find the hypotenuse.
~C. 300 ft#Multiplying the legs does not find a side length.
=D. 25 ft#Use the Pythagorean Theorem; the hypotenuse is 25 ft.
}

::GEO_U04_L07_Q05::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L07_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.5<br><br>Question:<br>A cable forms a right triangle with legs 10 ft and 24 ft. What is the hypotenuse length? {
=A. 26 ft#Use the Pythagorean Theorem; the hypotenuse is 26 ft.
~B. 34 ft#Adding the legs does not find the hypotenuse.
~C. 14 ft#Subtracting the legs does not find the hypotenuse.
~D. 240 ft#Multiplying the legs does not find a side length.
}

::GEO_U04_L07_Q06::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L07_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.5<br><br>Question:<br>A ladder is the hypotenuse of length 13 ft, and one leg is 5 ft. What is the other leg? {
~A. 8 ft#Subtracting side lengths ignores the squared relationship.
=B. 12 ft#Use b<sup>2</sup> = 13<sup>2</sup> - 5<sup>2</sup>; the missing leg is 12 ft.
~C. 18 ft#Adding lengths does not solve for a missing leg.
~D. &radic;194 ft#This adds squares even though the hypotenuse is already known.
}

::GEO_U04_L07_Q07::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L07_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.5<br><br>Question:<br>A fire escape is the hypotenuse of length 25 ft, and one leg is 7 ft. What is the other leg? {
~A. 18 ft#Subtracting side lengths ignores the squared relationship.
~B. 32 ft#Adding lengths does not solve for a missing leg.
=C. 24 ft#Use b<sup>2</sup> = 25<sup>2</sup> - 7<sup>2</sup>; the missing leg is 24 ft.
~D. &radic;674 ft#This adds squares even though the hypotenuse is already known.
}

::GEO_U04_L07_Q08::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L07_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.5<br><br>Question:<br>A support wire is the hypotenuse of length 17 ft, and one leg is 8 ft. What is the other leg? {
~A. 9 ft#Subtracting side lengths ignores the squared relationship.
~B. 25 ft#Adding lengths does not solve for a missing leg.
~C. &radic;353 ft#This adds squares even though the hypotenuse is already known.
=D. 15 ft#Use b<sup>2</sup> = 17<sup>2</sup> - 8<sup>2</sup>; the missing leg is 15 ft.
}

::GEO_U04_L07_Q09::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L07_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.5<br><br>Question:<br>A flagpole cable is the hypotenuse of length 29 ft, and one leg is 20 ft. What is the other leg? {
=A. 21 ft#Use b<sup>2</sup> = 29<sup>2</sup> - 20<sup>2</sup>; the missing leg is 21 ft.
~B. 9 ft#Subtracting side lengths ignores the squared relationship.
~C. 49 ft#Adding lengths does not solve for a missing leg.
~D. &radic;1241 ft#This adds squares even though the hypotenuse is already known.
}

::GEO_U04_L07_Q10::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L07_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.5<br><br>Question:<br>A tower brace is the hypotenuse of length 41 ft, and one leg is 9 ft. What is the other leg? {
~A. 32 ft#Subtracting side lengths ignores the squared relationship.
=B. 40 ft#Use b<sup>2</sup> = 41<sup>2</sup> - 9<sup>2</sup>; the missing leg is 40 ft.
~C. 50 ft#Adding lengths does not solve for a missing leg.
~D. &radic;1762 ft#This adds squares even though the hypotenuse is already known.
}

::GEO_U04_L07_Q11::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L07_Q11<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.5<br><br>Question:<br>A building height h is opposite a 35&deg; angle and the horizontal distance is 50 m. Which setup fits? {
~A. sin 35&deg; = <sup>50</sup>&frasl;<sub>h</sub>#Sine uses opposite over hypotenuse, and 50 is adjacent.
~B. cos 35&deg; = <sup>h</sup>&frasl;<sub>50</sub>#Cosine uses adjacent over hypotenuse.
=C. tan 35&deg; = <sup>h</sup>&frasl;<sub>50</sub>#Tangent uses opposite over adjacent.
~D. tan 35&deg; = <sup>50</sup>&frasl;<sub>h</sub>#This reverses opposite and adjacent.
}

::GEO_U04_L07_Q12::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L07_Q12<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.5<br><br>Question:<br>A ramp length r is the hypotenuse, and the rise is 4 ft with angle of elevation 20&deg;. Which setup uses sine? {
~A. cos 20&deg; = <sup>4</sup>&frasl;<sub>r</sub>#Cosine uses adjacent over hypotenuse.
~B. tan 20&deg; = <sup>4</sup>&frasl;<sub>r</sub>#Tangent uses opposite over adjacent, not hypotenuse.
~C. sin 20&deg; = <sup>r</sup>&frasl;<sub>4</sub>#This reverses sine.
=D. sin 20&deg; = <sup>4</sup>&frasl;<sub>r</sub>#Sine uses opposite over hypotenuse.
}

::GEO_U04_L07_Q13::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L07_Q13<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.5<br><br>Question:<br>A diagonal path crosses a 18 m by 24 m rectangular garden. What is the path length? {
=A. 30 m#The diagonal is the hypotenuse: 18<sup>2</sup> + 24<sup>2</sup> = 30<sup>2</sup>.
~B. 42 m#Adding side lengths does not find the diagonal.
~C. 6 m#Subtracting side lengths does not find the diagonal.
~D. 432 m#Multiplying side lengths gives area, not diagonal length.
}

::GEO_U04_L07_Q14::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L07_Q14<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.5<br><br>Question:<br>A kite string is 65 m long and the kite is 63 m above the ground. How far is the person horizontally from the point below the kite? {
~A. 2 m#Subtracting 63 from 65 ignores the squared relationship.
=B. 16 m#The string is the hypotenuse: x<sup>2</sup> + 63<sup>2</sup> = 65<sup>2</sup>, so x = 16.
~C. 128 m#Adding the lengths does not find the horizontal distance.
~D. &radic;8194 m#This adds squares even though 65 is the hypotenuse.
}

::GEO_U04_L07_Q15::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L07_Q15<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.5<br><br>Question:<br>A support cable makes a right triangle with opposite side 12 and adjacent side 35 relative to angle A. Which ratio finds angle A? {
~A. sin A = <sup>12</sup>&frasl;<sub>35</sub>#Sine uses opposite over hypotenuse.
~B. cos A = <sup>12</sup>&frasl;<sub>35</sub>#Cosine uses adjacent over hypotenuse.
=C. tan A = <sup>12</sup>&frasl;<sub>35</sub>#Tangent uses opposite over adjacent.
~D. tan A = <sup>35</sup>&frasl;<sub>12</sub>#This reverses tangent.
}

::GEO_U04_L07_Q16::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L07_Q16<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.5<br><br>Question:<br>A zipline has horizontal distance 80 ft and vertical drop 18 ft. Which theorem finds the zipline length? {
~A. AA similarity#No triangle similarity evidence is needed for this side-length problem.
~B. Reflection symmetry#No symmetry is involved.
~C. Scale factor only#No similar figure scale factor is given.
=D. Pythagorean Theorem#The horizontal and vertical distances are legs of a right triangle, so the hypotenuse is found with the Pythagorean Theorem.
}

::GEO_U04_L07_Q17::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L07_Q17<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.5<br><br>Question:<br>A right-triangle application gives an acute angle, adjacent side, and asks for hypotenuse. Which trig ratio uses adjacent and hypotenuse? {
=A. Cosine#Cosine is adjacent over hypotenuse.
~B. Sine#Sine uses opposite and hypotenuse.
~C. Tangent#Tangent uses opposite and adjacent.
~D. Pythagorean only#A trig ratio is appropriate because an angle is given.
}

::GEO_U04_L07_Q18::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L07_Q18<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.5<br><br>Question:<br>A surveyor knows the angle of elevation and horizontal distance to a tree. Which side is the tree height? {
~A. Adjacent side#The horizontal distance is adjacent to the angle.
=B. Opposite side#The height is across from the angle of elevation.
~C. Hypotenuse#The line of sight is the hypotenuse.
~D. Right angle#The height is a side length, not an angle.
}

::GEO_U04_L07_Q19::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L07_Q19<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.5<br><br>Question:<br>A 30-ft ladder reaches 24 ft up a wall. How far is the ladder base from the wall? {
~A. 6 ft#Subtracting 24 from 30 ignores squared lengths.
~B. 54 ft#Adding lengths does not solve the right triangle.
=C. 18 ft#The ladder is the hypotenuse: x<sup>2</sup> + 24<sup>2</sup> = 30<sup>2</sup>, so x = 18.
~D. &radic;1476 ft#This adds squares while treating both known values as legs.
}

::GEO_U04_L07_Q20::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L07_Q20<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.5<br><br>Question:<br>A diagonal of a square is 10&radic;2 units. What is the side length? {
~A. 20 units#This doubles the coefficient and does not reverse the diagonal relationship.
~B. 5 units#This halves the coefficient without using the &radic;2 relationship.
~C. 10&radic;2 units#That is the diagonal, not the side length.
=D. 10 units#For a square, the diagonal is side times &radic;2, so the side is 10.
}

::GEO_U04_L07_Q21::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L07_Q21<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.5<br><br>Question:<br>A right triangle has hypotenuse 50 and one leg 14. Which setup finds the other leg x? {
=A. x<sup>2</sup> + 14<sup>2</sup> = 50<sup>2</sup>#The known leg and missing leg squares add to the hypotenuse square.
~B. x<sup>2</sup> + 50<sup>2</sup> = 14<sup>2</sup>#This incorrectly treats 14 as the hypotenuse.
~C. x + 14 = 50#The Pythagorean relationship uses squares.
~D. 14x = 50#Multiplying a leg by the missing leg is not the theorem.
}

::GEO_U04_L07_Q22::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L07_Q22<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.5<br><br>Question:<br>A right-triangle problem gives opposite and adjacent sides and asks for an angle. Which ratio should be used? {
~A. Sine#Sine uses opposite and hypotenuse.
=B. Tangent#Tangent uses opposite and adjacent.
~C. Cosine#Cosine uses adjacent and hypotenuse.
~D. Scale factor#Scale factor is not the trig ratio for opposite and adjacent.
}

::GEO_U04_L07_Q23::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L07_Q23<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.5<br><br>Question:<br>Why should units be included in right-triangle application answers? {
~A. They change the triangle shape.#Units label measurements but do not change geometry.
~B. They replace calculations.#Calculations are still needed.
=C. They explain what the measurement represents.#Units connect the numerical answer back to the real context.
~D. They prove similarity.#Units do not prove similarity.
}

